const passages = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"passageNumber\":1,\"title\":\"The Bilingual Brain: Executive Function and Neural Plasticity\",\"subtitle\":\"How acquiring multiple languages physically alters neural architecture across the lifespan\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nFor much of the twentieth century, conventional pedagogical dogma maintained that childhood bilingualism was an intellectual hindrance. Early linguistic theorists posited that exposing an infant to two concurrent language systems would overload finite cognitive capacity, resulting in developmental speech delays and intellectual confusion. However, over the past three decades, a revolution in neuroimaging—spearheaded by functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) and magnetoencephalography—has dismantled this deficit model. Modern cognitive science demonstrates that actively managing two linguistic codes not only enriches communication, but physically alters brain structure and sharpens executive functioning.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nCentral to the cognitive benefits of bilingualism is the concept of non-selective lexical access. When a bilingual individual prepares to speak in Spanish, their brain does not cleanly deactivate their English vocabulary. Psycholinguistic eye-tracking experiments consistently prove that both language networks remain concurrently active in the cerebral cortex. Consequently, whenever bilinguals formulate an utterance, their neural apparatus must resolve persistent interference: suppressing the task-irrelevant language while activating the target vocabulary. This constant competition acts as continuous mental resistance training for the brain's executive control center, situated primarily in the prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate cortex.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThis daily mental gymnastics confers measurable advantages in domain-general cognitive flexibility. Bilingual children and adults routinely outperform monolingual peers on standardized non-verbal assessment paradigms, such as the dimensional change card sort and the Stroop test. These tasks evaluate attentional shifting, conflict monitoring, and inhibitory control—the capacity to ignore irrelevant sensory distractors while pursuing a goal. Because bilinguals spend every waking conversation actively filtering out cross-linguistic competition, they develop heightened cognitive reserves that translate directly into non-linguistic problem-solving scenarios.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nFurthermore, these functional cognitive adaptations correspond to structural alterations in brain anatomy. Structural MRI studies reveal that bilingual individuals exhibit greater gray matter volume in the left inferior parietal cortex—a region critically involved in vocabulary acquisition and phonological processing. Additionally, long-term bilingual practice strengthens white matter integrity, notably within the superior longitudinal fasciculus and corpus callosum. White matter constitutes the fatty myelin sheaths insulating neural axons; enhanced white matter ensures faster, more synchronized transmission of electrical signals between hemispheric processing lobes.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nPerhaps the most socially consequential revelation concerns healthy aging and neurodegenerative pathology. Epidemiological longitudinal surveys directed by Dr. Ellen Bialystok in Toronto established that lifelong bilinguals exhibit clinical symptoms of Alzheimer's disease and vascular dementia four to five years later than demographically matched monolinguals. Post-mortem histological examinations revealed a startling paradox: the brains of bilingual patients exhibited identical degrees of advanced amyloid-beta plaque accumulation and neural atrophy as monolingual patients, yet the bilingual individuals had continued living independently and conversing lucidly years after monolingual patients had succumbed to cognitive impairment. The bilingual brain's denser synaptic connectivity provides neuroprotective cognitive resilience, allowing it to bypass damaged pathways via alternative neural circuits.\",\"wordCount\":790,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 1–6 on your answer sheet, write:\\nTRUE if the statement agrees with the information\\nFALSE if the statement contradicts the information\\nNOT GIVEN if there is no information on this\",\"questionType\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a2-q1\",\"questionNumber\":1,\"prompt\":\"Mid-twentieth-century educators widely believed that learning two languages early could delay child development.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A notes that early theorists posited bilingualism would overload capacity, 'resulting in developmental speech delays and intellectual confusion.'\",\"evidence\":\"Early linguistic theorists posited that exposing an infant to two concurrent language systems would overload finite cognitive capacity, resulting in developmental speech delays and intellectual confusion.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q2\",\"questionNumber\":2,\"prompt\":\"When a bilingual person speaks one language, their other language system is entirely shut down.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states that non-selective lexical access means the brain does not deactivate the other language: 'both language networks remain concurrently active in the cerebral cortex.'\",\"evidence\":\"When a bilingual individual prepares to speak in Spanish, their brain does not cleanly deactivate their English vocabulary.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q3\",\"questionNumber\":3,\"prompt\":\"Bilingual speakers consistently require more time than monolinguals to complete the Stroop test.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C states bilinguals 'routinely outperform monolingual peers on standardized non-verbal assessment paradigms, such as the dimensional change card sort and the Stroop test.'\",\"evidence\":\"Bilingual children and adults routinely outperform monolingual peers on standardized non-verbal assessment paradigms, such as the dimensional change card sort and the Stroop test.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q4\",\"questionNumber\":4,\"prompt\":\"Increased white matter integrity enables more rapid electrical signal conduction across brain lobes.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states: 'enhanced white matter ensures faster, more synchronized transmission of electrical signals between hemispheric processing lobes.'\",\"evidence\":\"enhanced white matter ensures faster, more synchronized transmission of electrical signals between hemispheric processing lobes.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q5\",\"questionNumber\":5,\"prompt\":\"Dr. Ellen Bialystok's research focused primarily on children under six years old.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E mentions Bialystok's longitudinal surveys regarding Alzheimer's and dementia symptoms in older lifelong bilinguals, but does not state that her focus was on children under six.\",\"evidence\":\"Epidemiological longitudinal surveys directed by Dr. Ellen Bialystok in Toronto established that lifelong bilinguals exhibit clinical symptoms of Alzheimer's disease and vascular dementia four to five years later than demographically matched monolinguals.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q6\",\"questionNumber\":6,\"prompt\":\"Post-mortem tests showed bilingual patients had significantly fewer amyloid plaques than monolinguals.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E explicitly states that post-mortem exams showed 'identical degrees of advanced amyloid-beta plaque accumulation and neural atrophy'.\",\"evidence\":\"the brains of bilingual patients exhibited identical degrees of advanced amyloid-beta plaque accumulation and neural atrophy as monolingual patients\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 7–13 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"summary_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a2-q7\",\"questionNumber\":7,\"prompt\":\"Cognitive linguists use the term ____ to describe how multiple linguistic systems stay active at once.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"non-selective lexical access\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B introduces 'the concept of non-selective lexical access'.\",\"evidence\":\"Central to the cognitive benefits of bilingualism is the concept of non-selective lexical access.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q8\",\"questionNumber\":8,\"prompt\":\"The primary executive control center of the human brain is housed within the ____ cortex.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"prefrontal\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B refers to 'situated primarily in the prefrontal cortex'.\",\"evidence\":\"situated primarily in the prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate cortex.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q9\",\"questionNumber\":9,\"prompt\":\"Executive control skills include the ability to exercise ____ over competing linguistic inputs.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"inhibitory control\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C lists 'attentional shifting, conflict monitoring, and inhibitory control'.\",\"evidence\":\"These tasks evaluate attentional shifting, conflict monitoring, and inhibitory control\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q10\",\"questionNumber\":10,\"prompt\":\"Structural imaging shows higher volume of ____ in the left inferior parietal region.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"gray matter\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states: 'exhibit greater gray matter volume in the left inferior parietal cortex'.\",\"evidence\":\"Structural MRI studies reveal that bilingual individuals exhibit greater gray matter volume in the left inferior parietal cortex\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q11\",\"questionNumber\":11,\"prompt\":\"Axons are insulated by fatty structures called ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"myelin sheaths\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D: 'fatty myelin sheaths insulating neural axons'.\",\"evidence\":\"White matter constitutes the fatty myelin sheaths insulating neural axons\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q12\",\"questionNumber\":12,\"prompt\":\"Lifelong bilingualism can postpone the overt symptoms of Alzheimer's by approximately ____ years.\",\"correctAnswer\":[\"four to five\",\"4 to 5\",\"4-5\"],\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E: 'four to five years later than demographically matched monolinguals.'\",\"evidence\":\"lifelong bilinguals exhibit clinical symptoms of Alzheimer's disease and vascular dementia four to five years later\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q13\",\"questionNumber\":13,\"prompt\":\"The bilingual brain maintains functional capability by rerouting signals through ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"alternative neural circuits\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E ends with 'allowing it to bypass damaged pathways via alternative neural circuits.'\",\"evidence\":\"allowing it to bypass damaged pathways via alternative neural circuits.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":2,\"title\":\"Acoustic Ecology: Listening to the Pulse of Wilderness\",\"subtitle\":\"How autonomous bioacoustic sensor arrays are transforming tropical biodiversity conservation\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nAssessing biodiversity across vast, impenetrable equatorial rainforests has historically confronted field biologists with formidable logistical impediments. Traditional manual surveys—reliant on visual observation along cleared transects, physical mist-netting of avifauna, or intrusive capture-recapture protocols—are notoriously labor-intensive, hazardous, and temporally restricted. Moreover, human physical presence unavoidably disrupts animal behavior, driving elusive and endangered fauna into deeper canopy concealment. Over the past decade, however, the burgeoning discipline of passive acoustic monitoring (PAM) has revolutionized ecological surveillance.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nPassive acoustic monitoring deploys ruggedized autonomous recording units (ARUs) securely affixed to tree trunks throughout the rainforest understory and canopy strata. Weatherproofed and powered by photovoltaic solar panels or long-duration lithium-ion batteries, these devices continuously capture environmental soundscapes across months without human intervention. The recorded auditory tapestry is segregated by bioacousticians into three distinct acoustic spectra: biophony (the acoustic signals generated by living organisms, such as bird song, anuran calls, and stridulating insects); geophony (inanimate geophysical ambient noise, including wind, precipitation, and waterfalls); and anthrophony (human-generated acoustic disturbances, such as chainsaw motor whine, vehicular transit, and gunshots).\\n\\nParagraph C\\nBy scrutinizing the interplay between these acoustic components, researchers calculate aggregate biodiversity metrics without identifying individual vocalizations by hand. Chief among these mathematical indices is the Acoustic Complexity Index (ACI). Formulated by ecologist Dr. Almo Farina, the ACI measures the intensity variation across consecutive audio frequency bins. Because biophonic choruses produced by diverse bird communities fluctuate rapidly in amplitude, undisturbed habitats yield markedly high ACI values. Conversely, degraded tracts or monoculture oil palm plantations produce flat, monochromatic soundscapes that score poorly. In field trials across the Amazon and Borneo, ACI trends have correlated tightly with independently verified bird species richness.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nSimultaneously, advances in machine learning have enabled species-specific identification. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs), initially designed for image recognition, are repurposed to parse visual representations of sound known as audio spectrograms. By translating complex vocal signatures—such as the distinctive dawn calls of the critically endangered white-handed gibbon—into visual time-frequency patterns, algorithms can scan terabytes of continuous field recordings in minutes. In a landmark 2023 Bornean study led by Dr. Zuzana Burivalova, deep-learning models achieved a 94 percent precision rate in detecting rare primate alarm vocalizations, even amidst torrential tropical rainstorms.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nBeyond tracking biological community health, bioacoustic networks serve as real-time tactical alarms against illegal environmental crime. When acoustic sensors detect the high-frequency acoustic footprint of a running chainsaw or the transient acoustic pressure wave of a shotgun blast, the system leverages satellite or cellular uplinks to transmit automated GPS-tagged alerts directly to rangers' mobile handsets within thirty seconds. In Peru's Madre de Dios region, the deployment of canopy-mounted acoustic sentinels decreased illegal timber incursions by seventy-six percent within protected conservation concessions across an eighteen-month evaluation window.\\n\\nParagraph F\\nNonetheless, bioacoustics confronts substantial computational and logistical bottlenecks. Managing petabytes of high-resolution acoustic audio recorded across thousands of wilderness nodes requires significant cloud server infrastructure, which is economically inaccessible to underfunded global-south wildlife agencies. Furthermore, heavy tropical downpours and raging river geophony can drown out delicate animal calls, generating false negatives. Bioacousticians emphasize that while audio sensor arrays represent a transformative tool, they must augment, rather than entirely supplant, on-the-ground indigenous trackers and trained park rangers.\",\"wordCount\":810,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, A–F. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. Write the correct number, i–viii, in boxes 14–19 on your answer sheet.\\n\\nList of Headings\\ni. Mathematical formulas measuring general habitat complexity\\nii. Technical obstacles and financial limitations of sensor arrays\\niii. Inadequacies of legacy manual field census methods\\niv. Real-time detection and interception of illicit timber harvesting\\nv. Classifying environmental sounds into three distinct categories\\nvi. The commercial trade in captured wild songbirds\\nvii. Automated visual sound pattern recognition through AI\\nviii. Selective logging techniques that protect primate species\",\"questionType\":\"matching_headings\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a2-q14\",\"questionNumber\":14,\"prompt\":\"Paragraph A\",\"options\":[\"i\",\"ii\",\"iii\",\"iv\",\"v\",\"vi\",\"vii\",\"viii\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"iii\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A outlines the limitations of traditional manual surveys: labor-intensive, disruptive, and dangerous.\",\"evidence\":\"Traditional manual surveys—reliant on visual observation along cleared transects, physical mist-netting of avifauna, or intrusive capture-recapture protocols—are notoriously labor-intensive, hazardous...\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q15\",\"questionNumber\":15,\"prompt\":\"Paragraph B\",\"options\":[\"i\",\"ii\",\"iii\",\"iv\",\"v\",\"vi\",\"vii\",\"viii\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"v\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains dividing sounds into biophony, geophony, and anthrophony.\",\"evidence\":\"The recorded auditory tapestry is segregated by bioacousticians into three distinct acoustic spectra: biophony... geophony... and anthrophony\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q16\",\"questionNumber\":16,\"prompt\":\"Paragraph C\",\"options\":[\"i\",\"ii\",\"iii\",\"iv\",\"v\",\"vi\",\"vii\",\"viii\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"i\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C details the Acoustic Complexity Index (ACI) formulated by Dr. Almo Farina.\",\"evidence\":\"Chief among these mathematical indices is the Acoustic Complexity Index (ACI). Formulated by ecologist Dr. Almo Farina, the ACI measures the intensity variation across consecutive audio frequency bins.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q17\",\"questionNumber\":17,\"prompt\":\"Paragraph D\",\"options\":[\"i\",\"ii\",\"iii\",\"iv\",\"v\",\"vi\",\"vii\",\"viii\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"vii\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D covers machine learning, spectrograms, and convolutional neural networks.\",\"evidence\":\"Convolutional neural networks (CNNs), initially designed for image recognition, are repurposed to parse visual representations of sound known as audio spectrograms.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q18\",\"questionNumber\":18,\"prompt\":\"Paragraph E\",\"options\":[\"i\",\"ii\",\"iii\",\"iv\",\"v\",\"vi\",\"vii\",\"viii\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"iv\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E describes real-time alerts against illegal logging and poaching.\",\"evidence\":\"bioacoustic networks serve as real-time tactical alarms against illegal environmental crime. When acoustic sensors detect the high-frequency acoustic footprint of a running chainsaw...\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q19\",\"questionNumber\":19,\"prompt\":\"Paragraph F\",\"options\":[\"i\",\"ii\",\"iii\",\"iv\",\"v\",\"vi\",\"vii\",\"viii\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"ii\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F highlights computational bottlenecks, cloud costs, and noise interference.\",\"evidence\":\"Nonetheless, bioacoustics confronts substantial computational and logistical bottlenecks. Managing petabytes of high-resolution acoustic audio recorded across thousands of wilderness nodes requires significant cloud server infrastructure...\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Look at the following statements (Questions 20–23) and the list of researchers below. Match each statement with the correct researcher, A, B, or C. Write the correct letter in boxes 20–23.\",\"questionType\":\"matching_features\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a2-q20\",\"questionNumber\":20,\"prompt\":\"Developed a mathematical formula to assess habitat soundscape diversity without manual species tagging.\",\"options\":[\"A. Dr. Ellen Bialystok\",\"B. Dr. Almo Farina\",\"C. Dr. Zuzana Burivalova\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C states that Dr. Almo Farina formulated the Acoustic Complexity Index.\",\"evidence\":\"Chief among these mathematical indices is the Acoustic Complexity Index (ACI). Formulated by ecologist Dr. Almo Farina\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q21\",\"questionNumber\":21,\"prompt\":\"Demonstrated that deep-learning networks can recognize endangered gibbon alarm calls with 94% precision.\",\"options\":[\"A. Dr. Ellen Bialystok\",\"B. Dr. Almo Farina\",\"C. Dr. Zuzana Burivalova\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D cites the study led by Dr. Zuzana Burivalova in Borneo.\",\"evidence\":\"In a landmark 2023 Bornean study led by Dr. Zuzana Burivalova, deep-learning models achieved a 94 percent precision rate\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q22\",\"questionNumber\":22,\"prompt\":\"Correlated rapid acoustic amplitude variations with confirmed avian species counts in rainforests.\",\"options\":[\"A. Dr. Ellen Bialystok\",\"B. Dr. Almo Farina\",\"C. Dr. Zuzana Burivalova\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes Farina's ACI index correlated with bird richness in Amazon and Borneo.\",\"evidence\":\"Formulated by ecologist Dr. Almo Farina... In field trials across the Amazon and Borneo, ACI trends have correlated tightly with independently verified bird species richness.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q23\",\"questionNumber\":23,\"prompt\":\"Pioneered neural audio analysis specifically adapted to overcome severe weather interference.\",\"options\":[\"A. Dr. Ellen Bialystok\",\"B. Dr. Almo Farina\",\"C. Dr. Zuzana Burivalova\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states Burivalova's deep learning model detected calls 'even amidst torrential tropical rainstorms.'\",\"evidence\":\"led by Dr. Zuzana Burivalova, deep-learning models achieved a 94 percent precision rate in detecting rare primate alarm vocalizations, even amidst torrential tropical rainstorms.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D. Write the correct letter in boxes 24–26 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a2-q24\",\"questionNumber\":24,\"prompt\":\"What does the term 'geophony' refer to in bioacoustic science?\",\"options\":[\"Vocalizations made by subterranean burrowing insects\",\"Sound waves emitted by passing commercial aircraft\",\"High-frequency ultrasonic bat echolocation\",\"Non-biological physical sounds generated by weather and water\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B defines geophony as 'inanimate geophysical ambient noise, including wind, precipitation, and waterfalls'.\",\"evidence\":\"geophony (inanimate geophysical ambient noise, including wind, precipitation, and waterfalls)\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q25\",\"questionNumber\":25,\"prompt\":\"How quickly do modern canopy audio sentinels transmit gunshot alerts to wildlife rangers?\",\"options\":[\"Within thirty seconds\",\"Within twenty-four hours\",\"Approximately five minutes later\",\"At midnight during daily server synchronization\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E states: 'transmit automated GPS-tagged alerts directly to rangers' mobile handsets within thirty seconds.'\",\"evidence\":\"transmit automated GPS-tagged alerts directly to rangers' mobile handsets within thirty seconds.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q26\",\"questionNumber\":26,\"prompt\":\"What is the author's overall stance regarding the role of automated bioacoustic monitoring?\",\"options\":[\"It should entirely replace human park rangers and indigenous trackers.\",\"It is an exceptional tool that should complement human field staff rather than eliminate them.\",\"It is too unreliable during rainstorms to be of any practical use.\",\"It is only cost-effective in small municipal urban gardens.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F concludes: 'they must augment, rather than entirely supplant, on-the-ground indigenous trackers and trained park rangers.'\",\"evidence\":\"Bioacousticians emphasize that while audio sensor arrays represent a transformative tool, they must augment, rather than entirely supplant, on-the-ground indigenous trackers and trained park rangers.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":3,\"title\":\"Marine Ice Sheet Instability and Global Sea Levels\",\"subtitle\":\"Deciphering the retrograde slope dynamics and ice shelf pinning points of West Antarctica\",\"text\":\"Paragraph 1\\nThe vast ice reservoirs of the polar cryosphere exert a controlling influence on planetary albedo and global mean sea levels. While the melting of mountain glaciers contributes predictably to volumetric ocean expansion, glaciologists harbor grave concerns regarding the potential collapse of marine ice sheets—predominantly the West Antarctic Ice Sheet (WAIS). Unlike terrestrial ice sheets that rest securely on bedrock positioned well above sea level, marine-based ice sheets have bedrock foundations situated hundreds of meters below sea level. This unique morphological vulnerability renders them susceptible to a self-sustaining mechanical failure known as Marine Ice Sheet Instability (MISI).\\n\\nParagraph 2\\nThe stability of a marine ice sheet is anchored by its grounding line: the physical boundary where the grounded inland ice sheet detaches from bedrock and becomes a floating ice shelf over oceanic waters. Floating ice shelves act as colossal structural buttresses, exerting back-pressure that impedes the seaward discharge of tributary ice streams. However, warming circumpolar deep water (CDW) driven by shifting Southern Ocean wind patterns is increasingly penetrating sub-ice shelf cavities. This relatively warm ocean water melts the underside of floating ice shelves at rates far exceeding surface atmospheric melting, thinning the shelves and eroding the pinning points that anchor them against submerged shoals.\\n\\nParagraph 3\\nThe danger becomes acute when the grounding line retreats across a retrograde bed slope—a geological profile where the bedrock slopes downward toward the interior of the continent rather than upward toward the sea. As warm water melts the grounding line deeper into a retrograde basin, the thickness of the ice at the grounding line inevitably increases because the underlying bedrock is deeper. Under the physical laws of ice rheology, the seaward discharge of ice is directly proportional to ice thickness at the grounding line. Consequently, a deeper grounding line accelerates ice discharge into the sea, which pushes the grounding line even further inland, initiating an irreversible runaway feedback loop independent of future atmospheric warming.\\n\\nParagraph 4\\nGlaciological attention is centered acutely on Thwaites Glacier, colloquially dubbed the 'Doomsday Glacier'. Draining an ice basin larger than the United Kingdom, Thwaites holds enough ice to elevate global sea levels by over sixty-five centimeters directly. More critically, Thwaites serves as the linchpin protecting the interior WAIS basin; should it collapse, neighboring glaciers like Pine Island would follow, potentially unleashing over three meters of global sea level rise across several centuries. Autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) deployed beneath Thwaites have revealed complex thermal terracing: jagged crevasses where turbulent warm water currents circulate upwards, accelerating basal melt rates along mechanical shear zones.\\n\\nParagraph 5\\nPredicting the precise timeline of potential MISI collapse remains one of contemporary climate science's most daunting computational challenges. Numerical ice sheet models must simulate sub-ice oceanic turbulence, basal friction mechanics across slippery till beds, and calving physics simultaneously. While some conservative projections anticipate that catastrophic grounding line collapse would require several centuries, high-resolution coupled simulations indicate that ice cliff structural collapse (MICI)—where sheer ice cliffs exceeding one hundred meters collapse under their own gravitational stress once supporting shelves disintegrate—could compress this timescale into mere decades, fundamentally rewriting global coastal risk maps.\",\"wordCount\":840,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements reflect the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3? In boxes 27–32 on your answer sheet, write:\\nYES if the statement reflects the claims of the writer\\nNO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer\\nNOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this\",\"questionType\":\"yes_no_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a2-q27\",\"questionNumber\":27,\"prompt\":\"Marine ice sheets rest on bedrock that lies hundreds of meters beneath sea level.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"YES\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph 1 states: 'marine-based ice sheets have bedrock foundations situated hundreds of meters below sea level.'\",\"evidence\":\"marine-based ice sheets have bedrock foundations situated hundreds of meters below sea level. This unique morphological vulnerability renders them susceptible to a self-sustaining mechanical failure\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q28\",\"questionNumber\":28,\"prompt\":\"Surface atmospheric air melting is the primary driver of West Antarctic ice shelf thinning.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NO\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph 2 contradicts this: 'This relatively warm ocean water melts the underside of floating ice shelves at rates far exceeding surface atmospheric melting...'\",\"evidence\":\"melts the underside of floating ice shelves at rates far exceeding surface atmospheric melting, thinning the shelves and eroding the pinning points\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q29\",\"questionNumber\":29,\"prompt\":\"Once triggered on a retrograde bed slope, grounding line retreat can continue even if atmospheric temperatures stabilize.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"YES\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph 3 notes: 'initiating an irreversible runaway feedback loop independent of future atmospheric warming.'\",\"evidence\":\"which pushes the grounding line even further inland, initiating an irreversible runaway feedback loop independent of future atmospheric warming.\"},{\"id\":\"a2-q30\",\"questionNumber\":30,\"prompt\":\"Thwaites Glacier drains an area roughly equal in size to the state of California.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT